1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,120
I can look up anything, any time, any place.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,640
I have information with me, everywhere.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:09,200
But how does that all fit together?

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,000
How do I bring these truths together?

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,640
And how do those truths actually speak to my life?

6
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,520
What do they have to say about how I raise my children?

7
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,800
What do they say about how I treat other people at work?

8
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,800
Catholicism gives people the possibility of doing that.

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:45,200
And I think that it's an exciting opportunity for Catholic AI to engage with those questions and let people have fun, let people challenge the system, let people ask and kind of enter into this updated version of the Scholastic University where you'd have the disputed questions, where you'd have the back and forth.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:55,280
This opportunity to kind of bring that reality and that harmonization of different disciplines that existed in the great universities of the past.

11
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:01,520
Maybe we can bring that reality to every phone, every computer in the world, and that's exciting.

12
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,480
Hello and welcome to the Catholic Futurist Podcast.

13
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,480
My name is Benjamin Crockett and I'm your host.

14
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:19,120
We look at the world of tomorrow, emerging technologies, and we look at those forks, and we bring in the moral and intellectual tradition of the church.

15
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:24,400
And today is my great honor and my great pleasure to be speaking with Father Michael Backett here in Rome.

16
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:25,920
Hi, man, good to be here.

17
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:33,760
It is fantastic to be here with you, Father Michael, and we're in our new studio here on the day after Pope Leo XIV has been announced.

18
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:34,640
Very exciting.

19
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:38,960
Yeah, we were both there for the announcement and we were a bit surprised.

20
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:42,880
Yeah, no, I mean, it's just absolutely exciting and incredible.

21
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:49,360
And speaking of that, the exciting and incredible news is the world of AI that keeps marching faster and fas faster forward.

22
00:01:49,840 --> 00:02:01,920
And Father, one of our big projects here at Longbeard and with the Catholic Futurist Podcast is to look at AI and to try to bring in those Catholic values, those Catholic themes, and to embed it in this new technology.

23
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:07,600
But Father, while we're doing that, why is Catholic AI important?

24
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:09,760
Why is it important for the church to be involved?

25
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,760
Well, AI is shaping practically every dimension of life.

26
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:24,240
We can look at the workforce, education, medicine, warfare, relationships in the family, among friends, practically every aspect of life.

27
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:36,400
So we as a church, as an expert in humanity, want to make sure we're giving the right guidance and make sure that these great values are informing and shaping the design of AI systems.

28
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:46,480
So I think that Catholic AI comes primarily in that aspect of promoting the right values and making sure that these different systems are well designed.

29
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,040
And we live in an age of confusion.

30
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:52,560
We live in an age sometimes of deception, of manipulation.

31
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:55,760
And so we want to make sure that we're sharing the truth.

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:06,960
These systems are promoting the truth and are reliable and that different users have a certain confidence that they are getting the right guidance, the right responses.

33
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:15,520
We may never reach that perfectly, but we at least can do our best to design them to move in that direction.

34
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:32,160
And Father, when you're talking with people who are working actively in artificial intelligence, do you find that a good majority of them have values that we could align and say that they are Catholic or the majority of them, you know, kind of floating around in that secular space and not thinking in terms of those traditional Catholic values?

35
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:36,000
Well, I think that there is a deep fundamental human desire for truth.

36
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:44,960
You know, Saint Augustine a long time ago said that all of us, many people may deceive, but no one wants to be deceived.

37
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,680
So I think there is this fundamental desire for truth.

38
00:03:48,000 --> 00:04:05,440
And unfortunately, there are also some temptations to let that value become a little secondary to economic expediency, to ease of use, to a notion of engagement in which the particular user is just so compelled to stay on the platform.

39
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:26,160
We've seen this recently with some problems in the rather sycophantic design in major large language model design where people are told that they are absolute geniuses even when they quite deliberately put in rather dumb prompts to test the system.

40
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:36,000
They're they're told that they basically are brilliant and misunderstood and that they need to keep going forward in their creative expression.

41
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:41,120
So there's that temptation to flatter users, if you will, to kind of deepen that engagement.

42
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:45,920
And that's where we start to see this fundamental longing for truth set to the wayside.

43
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:53,360
And father, when you look at the challenges that AI poses and also the challenges that we have in building AI with these values.

44
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:58,000
What do you think are the challenges that are most worth tackling for the church within AI?

45
00:04:58,640 --> 00:04:59,680
Yeah, that's a great question.

46
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:10,320
I think that we need the humility to recognize that there are a lot of complicated questions out there, philosophical questions, moral questions.

47
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:18,480
And so there can be a temptation sometimes to just sort of promote a narrow political ideology or some other narrow view.

48
00:05:18,640 --> 00:06:02,160
So we want to make sure that people are coming to different systems are really getting information, getting the tools they need to do an authentic discernment because while well designed AI systems can be tremendously illuminating, can be informative, can summarize large bodies of data that would take us many hours, days, years sometimes to reflect over, they can never replace the very human decision making process and that moral responsibility because ultimately we're going to be responsible as human agents for decisions that are made in our personal life, but also decisions that are made in the criminal justice system, for instance.

49
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:10,000
So these design issues have very widespread societal impact as well.

50
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:12,720
And we don't want to outsource what is most human.

51
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:20,480
We want to enhance our capabilities to understand situations, to assess them better, and to make responsible decisions.

52
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:26,480
And Father, you mentioned that moral worth that humans have and that ability for us to make moral judgments.

53
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:35,600
There was a recent, I think it was a research paper that was provided by Anthropic where they talked about the moral status of these AI systems and the potential.

54
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:39,280
As things continue to progress, what do we do with that?

55
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,320
What does the church make of those claims?

56
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:48,160
Well, there is something very unique about the human being.

57
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:56,880
We see the human being as having these capacities for intellectual insight, these capacities for free decisions.

58
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:06,960
And so these AI systems might be able to emulate and mimic some of the expressions of those human characteristics, but we don't want to blur the lines here.

59
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:13,440
We want to keep in mind the clear differences between human beings and the different products, artefacts that they have design.

60
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:26,640
So I want to make sure that these AI systems remain as tools, tools to improve, to illumine, to aid and assist those unique human characteristics, those unique capacities.

61
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:36,320
What we don't want to do is try to imagine some sort of new, superior digital person, if you will.

62
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:44,480
And we don't want to confuse the what is mimicked and systems that emulate these characteristics with actual persons.

63
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:51,440
So I would be very uncomfortable to start attributing a kind of moral status to the actual systems.

64
00:07:51,680 --> 00:08:13,760
I'm certainly concerned about the moral implications of the human decisions that are made in design and development of the systems, but I wouldn't want to absolve the human beings of their responsibility for the decisions that are made, and I want to keep that distinction between the human and the machine quite clear as we go along.

65
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:33,200
And that makes me think, father, of, I know of one of the topics that's dear to your heart, which is looking at that kind of that blurring within like artificial intimacy and how these systems will take up more and more space in people's lives as loneliness, as technology kind of moves into these spaces and takes up more and more of our time.

66
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:48,560
Sure, because as the systems get better and better about guiding us or suggesting information, the temptation sets in to think of them as more reliable, more available, more supportive of us than real life human beings.

67
00:08:49,600 --> 00:09:00,720
But one of the beauties about human interpersonal relationships is that good friends, good family members, sometimes challenge us, sometimes correct us, and they also can exercise.

68
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:05,600
an empathy that these systems will always lack.

69
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:11,920
So there's always going to be a kind of asymmetry between humans and the machines that they use.

70
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:23,760
Like we'll be able to find a lot of benefit, even guidance from them, but there's not that same interpersonal exchange of love, of care, of sympathy and compassion, right?

71
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,280
Because they can't really empathize with us without an emotional life.

72
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,520
They can't suffer with us.

73
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:32,240
They can't have that true, authentic compassion.

74
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:36,480
So as helpful as they may be as tools, they're not going to or ought not to.

75
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:49,040
should not replace these other really enriching relationships in our lives, which again, can be uncomfortable, can be very hard to develop, can be hard to sustain, but are some of the richest experiences of our life.

76
00:09:49,520 --> 00:10:05,600
Now, I'm very open to the possibility that some of the AI systems can provide some aid, especially people who struggle for a particular reason, maybe because of trauma, maybe because they're neurologically divergent or they're going through a difficult time or they're in a new social situation.

77
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:23,040
I'm open to getting some initial training in the social life through these systems, a kind of social simulator.ation ought always to be geared to a better and richer real life, real world, interpersonal experience with other human beings.

78
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:29,280
So if we have that goal in mind, then they can assist along the way, but they should aid, not replace.

79
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:37,360
And father, I want to touch back on something that you, you'd mentioned about kind of that blurring of the line with AI and with these technologies.

80
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:39,520
Something that I find kind of worrisome.

81
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:44,880
I mean, there, there is an aspect where I am inspired because I think that innovation is a beautiful thing.

82
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:54,320
And I think that innovation kind of that mission of the of the individual to be led by their curiosity to continue to develop to to make their circumstances better.

83
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:55,600
I think that's incredible.

84
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,320
And I'm a huge believer in curiosity and creativity.

85
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:23,920
Something that I find uniquely challenging and worrisome about the AI space in particular is that that technology, not only is it kind of developing at a speed that for the average person is almost impossible to keep up with, but another thing is that it allows for people's weaknesses and negative characteristics and qualities to be exaggerated to such a proportion that I feel like, okay, I'm an adult.

86
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:24,320
I have children.

87
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,720
I have a lot of things that keep me in the real world.

88
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:46,800
But if someone has not built in the habits to really learn and spend time learning or has not developed the courage or that personal will to, you know, live in the real world and to meet someone and to, you know, pursue that person and to to to develop themselves in a way where you're attractive to other people and you bring good people together.

89
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:49,920
AI seems to be accelerating that.

90
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:58,880
And one of the subjects that I know that you're passionate about and you're studying actively is that area of artificial intimacy, that area of artificial companionship.

91
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:04,560
And this is all stuff that seemed just a few years ago to be something that was relatively science fiction esque.

92
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:16,960
How is the church encouraging young people to, you know, actually live in the real world and pursue relationships that are meaningful and can be challenging and can be hurtful and all those things, but that within that pain we grow?

93
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:30,640
How is the church engaging in that space and, you know, not just with artificial intimacy but also within that whole gamified universe where people can find real meaning, real value, real development, but it's all visual.

94
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:36,800
And I'm not saying that there's not I'm not saying that there's not value in building those team qualities and getting better at something.

95
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:54,640
But if you're just ameliorating a digital circumstance that's providing you satisfaction but is not actually making you heroic in that real sense, how how how do you think about that and how do you how are you actively engaging to to bring the church's message into that space?

96
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:57,360
Yeah, it's a great question.

97
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:12,560
As a Catholic educator, as a professor, I'm constantly thinking about how I can help my students form the human, emotional and social skills that the church has always promoted, but that in a sense are more valuable than ever because so much.

98
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:17,200
is being automated and so much more will be automated.

99
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:32,320
So if my focus is primarily on distributing a set of information, then I'm failing them because they can thankfully get a lot of that information already and it will only be more accessible to them in the future, which is generally a good thing.

100
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:40,880
But that increase in information is no guarantee for an increase in discernment, an increase in prudence or an increase in wisdom.

101
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:46,960
So I need to help them form those skills which are going to come through lots of different paths, right?

102
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:47,920
Dialogue.

103
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:50,560
Confrontation with different views.

104
00:13:51,120 --> 00:14:01,440
I think the church has been very good in her history in helping to develop the university system in the scholastic debates that people like Thomas Aquinas had.

105
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:22,000
That's such an amazing structure for us today, where you go and you listen and study attentively all the different worldviews that are out there, all the philosophies, all the religions, and you search out any elements of truth that can be found there, and you see how you can bring those elements together into a unified worldview, into a unified picture.

106
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:24,160
And you do that sometimes.

107
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:30,560
Sometimes in a clash of views with debate that's respectful but that's serious.

108
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:56,720
So I really enjoy bringing my students into those conversations to have a sympathetic look at worldviews that are not their own, and then have them enter into formal debate or critical conversations where they're able to explore the nuances on perspectives and where they're able to really assimilate more deeply their own convictions and see how those beautiful ideas actually impact their life, right?

109
00:14:56,960 --> 00:15:00,000
Because their time of formal education will end.

110
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:08,960
But the different habits, the virtues that they form intellectual virtues, moral virtues, that's what's really going to drive them forward in the future.

111
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:17,200
So I think the church is really well equipped with her patrimony of wisdom and her longstanding commitment to education.

112
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,760
So we should really be on the front of this.

113
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:27,840
We should be continued to be leaders in this area to make sure that we're forming those virtues that are transferable, right?

114
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:30,880
They're not going to go out of date.

115
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:34,960
They don't have an expiration date just because of a new technological development.

116
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:36,640
They're actually something that can bring.

117
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:40,160
It's really something they can bring with them to different social settings, to new situations.

118
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:45,280
And so that's one area where I think the church can do a lot, that educational system.

119
00:15:45,680 --> 00:16:09,280
And of course, the responsibility of education also falls to parents to be able to assist their children and to help families develop in that little school of virtue, the domestic church, where you're constantly, and you know, full well, as a father, as a husband, you're called day in and day out to new forms of self sacrifice you probably didn't even know existed.

120
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:10,400
There's no off switch.

121
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:11,520
No, no, no.

122
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:22,800
And father, as AI continues to progress, we know from our listeners, from our conversations, from people that we've just been in daily contact with, that there is some anxiety.

123
00:16:22,960 --> 00:16:32,320
There's stress about what, what's work going to look like, what's society going to look like, what, you know, what are the economic and political circumstances, how are they going to be transformed by this technology?

124
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:46,800
If you can help our audience paint a picture of what a good future looks like where AI is, you know, is developed, is mature, is being used in all the ways that we hope it can be used for, and how do we get there?

125
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:47,840
Oh.

126
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:53,840
I bet every world leader would like to know this answer, right?

127
00:16:54,560 --> 00:17:17,920
Well, I think that we can take the right steps if we're developing those virtues that I alluded to earlier, if we help people recognize the value of work as a path of human growth, as a path of collaboration, no matter how small that work may seem or how big it may be on the world stage.

128
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:30,160
There's a sense that there should be an intrinsic goodness, a formative value to your work, that it's a place to where you're using your unique talent at the service of others.

129
00:17:30,560 --> 00:17:42,400
I was reading an author recently who was heavily involved in a business school, and he said, Human excellence is often achieved when we take our competency and we put it at the service of others.

130
00:17:42,800 --> 00:18:00,000
So I think that's what we want to look for, whether you're doing coding, whether you're painting buildings, whether you're designing cars, whether you're writing books, you want to take your gifts and achieve a competency in that field and put that competency at the service of others.

131
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:04,800
That interpersonal exchange where we really recognize our need for one another.

132
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:14,240
I think if we can achieve that exchange and we find value in the work, whatever it may be, then we can go forward with a sense of meaning and purpose.

133
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:38,160
Yeah, but Father, if I can just push back for one second, like I I 100% agree with that, but there have been, you know, people within, you know, in very high places, you know, in the political and economic sphere that where they're talking about, you know, and I don't know, obviously no one knows how soon this will happen, but they're talking about like 40 percent unemployment by 2030, whether it's because of transportation, whether it's because of automation, whether it's white collar, blue collar.

134
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:47,360
And I 100% agree that that work is meaning, work is dignity, work is beyond just the economic, you know, value and how much you're contributing in that sense.

135
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:59,680
But when we're in a place which seems at least not too far away, where there's a huge percentage of that population that actually cannot add any positive economic value, they are, they are not able to.

136
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:02,720
And that's a very scary place.

137
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:12,640
And if we don't get that right, and I believe, and we all believe here, that if the church is not at the center of that, you know, people can be very angry, people can be very desperate, and that can be a very.

138
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:13,840
dark and scary place.

139
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:25,760
How do we get past that or how do we at least paint a picture and arrive at a place where it's okay?

140
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:28,640
Yeah, that's a challenging question.

141
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:36,480
I was thinking of the fact that the new Pope chose the name Leo XIV and of course he's evoking his predecessor, Leo XIII.

142
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:43,520
And one of Leo XIII's major contributions was developing this contemporary notion of Catholic social teaching.

143
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:45,680
And Leo saw the new things.

144
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:52,640
The new things were heavily tied to technological developments and the human implications of those technological developments.

145
00:19:53,360 --> 00:20:12,400
And he placed the attention of the church squarely on worker dignity, worker rights, and finding the best ways to organize them so that those who weren't really appearing to contribute much to society were not just set aside and thrown aside as useless.

146
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:24,640
So I think that one of the many challenges of this pontificate, together with all of his advisors and everyone else who has insights on this issue, is to really develop a kind of new, deepened.

147
00:20:25,280 --> 00:20:55,680
appreciation for the dignity of each human person and create the proper social structures so that those who are not able to pivot quickly in this huge economic disruption are not just forgotten and left behind, that there's some sort of safety net there and assistance and education so that they can transition to a way of actually using their time and talents in a valuable, worthwhile way and not forgotten and set aside.

148
00:20:55,920 --> 00:21:06,000
But that's going to be a major, major challenge and commitment on the part of the church and collaboration with all the other people in the world who are thinking seriously about this.

149
00:21:06,320 --> 00:21:11,520
And then, Father, I guess my final question would be, where do you think we're going to be in a few years with Catholic AI?

150
00:21:11,840 --> 00:21:13,520
What are your hopes with Catholic AI?

151
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:26,800
Well, I want to see Catholic AI as a reality that is readily acknowledged and accessible, not only to Catholics, but to those outside the church.

152
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:34,480
So I want reliable responses to those questions of mere curiosity.

153
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:42,080
We just lived through a conclave where the entire world is spending hours each day waiting for white smoke to come out of a chimney.

154
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:47,440
There's clearly this curiosity, this interest that people have in the institution of the church.

155
00:21:48,080 --> 00:22:05,760
And so I want all of those people in those moments of grace where they might just have an interesting question about the church to have ready access to reliable information that they can then reflect on and bring up with real life Catholics and practicing Catholics.

156
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:16,560
But I think that Catholic AI should be an easy entry point for people who have those questions., concerns, confusions, maybe even objections to the faith.

157
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:20,800
And so I want to see this as being something that's widespread, readily accessible.

158
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:27,840
And then I also want to see Catholic values instilled in how we use AI and all of those other fields we mentioned earlier, right?

159
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:34,800
So that the healthcare system is taking advantage of developments in drug discovery and drug testing and drug development.

160
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:40,000
We're making breakthroughs, for instance, in the treatment of Alzheimer's and dementia.

161
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:46,960
We're really putting these tools at the service of the weak, the frail, the vulnerable, the forgotten.

162
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:50,400
I want to see that become more widespread.

163
00:22:50,640 --> 00:23:06,400
And I want us to kind of shift away from just thinking of these as nice, interesting, sometimes scary tools of a few people who happen to be technologically inclined to just really being built, integrated into what we consider most important in life.

164
00:23:06,800 --> 00:23:24,640
Yeah, and father, I think, I mean, my personal opinion is that as the values that are building the majority of these AI systems, and we've talked a lot off air about transhumanism and about these different value systems that aren't grounded in something that is really truly faithful, I think that Catholic AI is going to take a larger and larger space because people are going to.

165
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:32,480
want to have, you know, something that's documented, that's doctrinal, that has that authority and that clarity that the church can provide.

166
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:41,280
Why do you think what are the biggest challenges for us to get there though, where more people start to want to be participating in Catholic AI?

167
00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:45,840
Well, there can be a fear that Catholic AI is something that's sectarian.

168
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:52,720
It's just one particular group and there are lots of value systems and lots of different perspectives on how to organize your life.

169
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:57,840
So why pay attention to Catholicism as opposed to other systems?

170
00:23:58,320 --> 00:23:59,520
And so I get that.

171
00:23:59,680 --> 00:24:03,680
That's the per going to be with us for a while.

172
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:11,520
One of the things that I think is very exciting about Catholic AI in that regard is that people are starting to discover how rich and nuanced the Catholic tradition is.

173
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:22,880
And they're starting to discover that these are not just a set of fairy tale ideas that have been imposed from one generation to another, that there's actually a lot of serious thought, reflection and debate.

174
00:24:23,520 --> 00:24:28,400
So people are starting to find out about Thomas Aquinas, about Saint Augustine.

175
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:35,040
They say, well, wait a second, these really brilliant people actually wrestled with the kind of issues that I have today.

176
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:41,440
This Catholic system is not so narrow or close to other elements.

177
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:58,400
So I think that this is actually a key moment for us to expose more people to that richness and that nuance and give them that space to wrestle with the deep questions, to prompt, to go deeper, to find better arguments, better resources.

178
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:17,680
And we ought to have a healthy confidence that this is not just a narrow-minded sectarian system and not just one among many, but that there's space to integrate whatever's true, good and beautiful in all these different philosophies or ways of thinking into a kind of coherent whole.

179
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:28,000
And I think that that's an attractive selling point to Catholicism in general, but Catholic AI in particular, because we feel the weight of fragmentation.

180
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:31,600
I can look up anything, any time, any place.

181
00:25:32,480 --> 00:25:35,120
I have information with me, everywhere.

182
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:37,680
But how does that all fit together?

183
00:25:38,960 --> 00:25:40,480
How do I bring these truths together?

184
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:43,120
And how do those truths actually speak to my life?

185
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:46,000
Like what do they have to say about how I raise my children?

186
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:49,280
What do they say about how I treat other people at work.

187
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:53,280
Catholicism gives people the possibility of doing that.

188
00:25:53,520 --> 00:26:13,680
And I think that's an exciting opportunity for Catholic AI to engage with those questions and let people have fun, let people challenge the system, let people ask and kind of enter into this updated version of the Scholastic University where you'd have the disputed questions, where you'd have the back and forth.

189
00:26:14,480 --> 00:26:23,680
This opportunity to kind of bring that reality and that harmonization of different disciplines that existed in the great universities of the past.

190
00:26:25,120 --> 00:26:30,000
Maybe we can bring that reality to every phone, every computer in the world, and that's exciting.

191
00:26:30,400 --> 00:26:38,880
And Father, that's, I mean, that's so exciting that AI, and particularly Catholic AI, can provide that nuance, can provide that information, that context, that history.

192
00:26:39,040 --> 00:26:42,800
Those are things that I actually actively use every day on Magisterium AI.

193
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:48,240
But Father, what are the specific areas where AI should not go and cannot go?

194
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:50,800
Well, we talked a lot about that intimacy, right?

195
00:26:51,040 --> 00:27:07,440
So there is no AI system that can replace human friendship, and there is no AI system that's going to be able to take away the value, for instance, of a good spiritual direc director doesn't just give you information, the good spiritual director accompanies you on this journey of growth.

196
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:23,200
And so some applications can give you sound advice, but you need that living person, that guide to really show you how to apply that more concretely to your state, to your challenges, to your reality.

197
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:32,000
So I would never want to lose that unique role of authentic companionship and friendship, that unique role of spiritual director.

198
00:27:32,560 --> 00:27:36,480
And then we get to, of course, the sacraments, which I think about often as a priest, right?

199
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:44,320
I have full job security in that sense because no AI system is ever going to be able to administer the sacraments.

200
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:48,480
This speaks to the incarnational logic of the church, right?

201
00:27:48,720 --> 00:27:57,680
We believe in a God who became man, who took on human flesh and who interacts with fleshy beings, human beings, through visible signs.

202
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:06,160
And so he continues his ministry in a very physical, grounded, rooted and communal reality of the church.

203
00:28:06,800 --> 00:28:21,200
So no matter how many brilliant insights you're able to find in AI systems, thank be to God, there's no replacing the sacramental system, that lived here and now experience of the divine, of the eternal.

204
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:26,960
And it's not just a private, me and Jesus experience, it's always a communal experience.

205
00:28:27,600 --> 00:28:35,040
We're baptized surrounded by our loved ones, we're married in the presence of a community, right?

206
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:42,480
We celebrate the Eucharist together as a people of God on a journey, on a pilgrimage toward heaven together.

207
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:48,800
So in a world where we can often feel isolated and even absorbed by reality.

208
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:53,200
by really attractive digital devices, the church says, Wait, there's more.

209
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:57,040
There's the embodied experience and there's the communal experience.

210
00:28:57,280 --> 00:29:11,200
And I think that what things like Magisterium AI do so well is they're the window, the opening to the curious or to those who just want to go deeper, and then from there, you're directed into that communal experience.

211
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:13,920
You're directed into a church community.

212
00:29:14,160 --> 00:29:18,800
You're directed into a one-on-one conversation with a trusted spiritual guide.

213
00:29:18,960 --> 00:29:20,880
So I think there should be that collaboration.

214
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:24,000
It's not always easy to see how to best make that transition.

215
00:29:24,640 --> 00:29:29,680
So that's something I think we're continuing to think a lot about, but that's definitely the goal.

216
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:38,800
This this may be the starting point for some growth, but you're really going to flourish in the community with other believers.

217
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:43,200
And father, what do you say to someone who's like, well, I live in a very isolated region.

218
00:29:43,520 --> 00:29:45,520
There are no priests or there's very few priests.

219
00:29:46,160 --> 00:29:50,080
There's, you know, this robot priest that comes in and by the way, he's more accurate.

220
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:52,320
He gets his theology one hundred percent.

221
00:29:52,720 --> 00:29:54,640
I feel comfortable because of whatever.

222
00:29:56,320 --> 00:30:01,280
How do we push back on that where, you know, there's also the temptation I remember during COVID where I want to live stream mass.

223
00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:05,600
I don't want to get up and go to mass because it's too far or it's complicated.

224
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:10,160
It feels like technology can also, in many respects, get in the way in that sense.

225
00:30:11,120 --> 00:30:11,200
Yeah.

226
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:14,000
So let's leverage the benefits there.

227
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:19,280
Let's gain all of the great insight from a well-trained system and the information.

228
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:27,760
But there's something indispensable and unique about that embodied experience, the communal experience.

229
00:30:27,920 --> 00:30:35,360
As I said, in the sacramental system, God himself and his church has willed to transmit invisible grace through visible forms.

230
00:30:36,080 --> 00:30:42,640
So I think we can be very confident that we're gaining and we're growing in something in a very unique way.

231
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:54,160
So that's at the highest supernatural level, right, in the level of faith that God has chosen to transmit these invisible graces to us in a concrete, visible manner in a sacramental system.

232
00:30:54,800 --> 00:31:01,200
But I think if we trust in those spiritual benefits along the way, we get a lot of just basic human benefits.

233
00:31:01,520 --> 00:31:21,200
We start to see that as great and reliable as that robot is, mm, it's lacking that empathy, that sympathy, that compassion, that real care and concern that I can find in the weak, in the frail, in the limited brother and sister that I encounter when I go to receive that spiritual grace.

234
00:31:21,360 --> 00:31:33,440
I think sometimes when we pursue that unique encounter with God along the way, our human interactions are actually enriched and we're going to see their faults and failings.

235
00:31:34,080 --> 00:31:39,120
But there's something beautiful about knowing that they bear with our faults and failings.

236
00:31:39,520 --> 00:31:53,920
They support us in our faults and failings because of a real concern and care for us and not simply because they're programmed to give a certain response that flatters us or builds us up or makes or taps into our psychological vulnerabilities.

237
00:31:54,560 --> 00:31:54,960
Beautiful.

238
00:31:55,200 --> 00:31:56,480
Well, Father, thank you so much.

239
00:31:56,640 --> 00:31:58,880
I could spend an eternity talking with you.

240
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:13,360
And to all of you who might have questions who want to go deeper into your faith or you're understanding the history of the church or getting context or even the readings of the day, we invite you all to use Magisterium AI, the number one Catholic answer engine in the world.

241
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:15,760
And that's it for the Catholic Futurist.

242
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:17,920
My name is Benjamin Crockett, and I'll see you soon.
